    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d670">

                <pb facs="0742" n="730" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg730"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890G.42/48</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister Resident in Iraq</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Knabenshue</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1542</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Baghdad</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-04-12">April 12,
                        1940</date>.<lb/>[Received June 1.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to my despatch
                    no. 1537 of April 8, 1940, regarding the proposed new Iraqi Education Law and to
                    inform the Department that in consequence of the action taken by this and <pb
                        facs="0743" n="731" xml:id="pg731"/> the French Legation and the British
                    Embassy the proposed law was withdrawn from the Iraqi Parliament by the Ministry
                    of Education for, it was announced in the press, the Ministry desired to make
                    certain basic changes. Subsequently I received an acknowledgement from the
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs of my note of April 6th,<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d670-f1"/> with the statement that “I assure Your Excellency that
                    the appropriate Iraqi authorities are attending to this matter in all its
                    aspects and shall communicate the result thereof to you in the near future.”</p>
                <p>While the present Iraqi Government seems disposed apparently to meet our recent
                    representations in a favorable manner, it is not impossible that we may be
                    obliged to cope with a similar situation in the future. The Department will of
                    course be kept informed of developments.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>P. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Knabenshue</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
